/**
 * The set of ground control points that relate real-world coordinates to a
 * {@link Floor}'s floor plan pixel space.
 *
 * This is a plain value type embedded in {@link Floor#calibration} — it has no
 * identity of its own and is not independently persistable.
 *
 * ### How the transform is fitted
 *
 * The number of points in {@link GeoCalibration#points} determines which
 * transform the client fits:
 *
 * - **3 or more points** — a full 6-parameter affine transform, solved by
 *   least squares. This absorbs rotation, independent x/y scaling, and shear,
 *   which matters because facilities drawings are frequently rotated relative
 *   to true north and are not always square.
 * - **exactly 2 points** — a 4-parameter similarity transform (uniform scale +
 *   rotation + translation). Correct when the drawing is uniformly scaled, but
 *   it cannot represent shear or anisotropic scale.
 * - **fewer than 2 points** — not solvable; the client must skip the GPS
 *   suggestion entirely and require a manual pin.
 *
 * ### Accuracy expectations
 *
 * Even a perfectly fitted transform only maps the *device's reported position*
 * into pixel space, and indoors that position typically carries 5-15 m of error
 * (far worse in building cores, and unavailable in basements). The transform
 * therefore produces a **suggestion plus an uncertainty radius**, never an
 * authoritative location. Choose control points that are far apart and easy to
 * identify on both the plan and a satellite map — opposite building corners are
 * ideal, because closely spaced points amplify fitting error dramatically.
 */
type GeoCalibration {

  /**
   * Two or more control points tying lat/lon to pixel xy on this floor.
   *
   * Order is not significant. See the type-level documentation for how point
   * count selects the transform model.
   */
  points: ![CalibrationPoint]
}
